-- title:
--   G Comm Stability Slice SSC
-- statement:
--   **Slice strong self-consistency follows from the family hypothesis.**
--
--   Let `params` be the test parameters, `strategy` a symmetric projective strategy, and `family` an indexed polynomial submeasurement. Let `G` satisfy `hG`, namely $G^x$ is the conversion of the measurement of `family` at height $x$. If `family` is strongly self-consistent with error $\zeta$ under `hself`, then the slice family has bipartite strong self-consistency with half that error:
--
--   $$
--   \operatorname{bipartiteSSCError}\left(\psi,\operatorname{Unif}(\mathbb{F}_q),G\right)\le\frac{\zeta}{2}.
--   $$
--
--   The factor one half comes from projectivity, which identifies the bipartite overlap defect with half of the corresponding left-right squared-distance defect. This transfers slice self-consistency to the evaluated point family.
--
--   Formalization credit: Sirui Lu, Ruixuan Deng, Yanqiao Zhu, and Zhengfeng Ji (arXiv:2609.19814). Mathematical source: Zhengfeng Ji, Anand Natarajan, Thomas Vidick, John Wright, and Henry Yuen (arXiv:2009.12982).
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The source declaration is `MIPStarRE.LDT.Commutativity.gCommStability_sliceSSC`; its Lean statement gives the exact quantifier order and all boundary hypotheses.
